{
  /* The desktop package cannot use the root tsconfig, which is why the root EXCLUDES it — and why
     nothing here was typechecked at all until an unbound identifier shipped through `bun run
     typecheck` unnoticed. CI runs this config as a second step for that reason.

     The cause is that electrobun's `exports` map points at raw TypeScript (`dist/api/bun/index.ts`,
     not a `.d.ts`), so importing it pulls its sources into the program as first-class files.
     Neither `exclude` nor `skipLibCheck` keeps them out — see the root config for why — and under
     the root's `exactOptionalPropertyTypes` they emit errors we cannot fix in a dependency.

     ONE of the root's flags is genuinely blocked. Measured on electrobun 0.x, today:

       exactOptionalPropertyTypes  → 14 errors across 6 electrobun files (BrowserView, BrowserWindow,
                                     Updater, Utils, Tray, ContextMenu, ApplicationMenu). Ours: 7.
       noUncheckedIndexedAccess    → 0 errors in electrobun. Ours: 129, ~90% of them array indexing
                                     in tests (88 in window-manager.test.ts alone).

     So `noUncheckedIndexedAccess` is off for OUR debt, not for the dependency's, and re-enabling it
     is a bounded piece of work rather than a wait. Prefer narrowing this file back toward the root
     config over widening it, and re-measure before believing either number. */
  "compilerOptions": {
    "allowImportingTsExtensions": true,
    "lib": ["ESNext", "DOM"],
    "module": "ESNext",
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    "noEmit": true,
    "noFallthroughCasesInSwitch": true,
    "noImplicitOverride": true,
    "resolveJsonModule": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "strict": true,
    "target": "ESNext",
    "types": ["bun-types"]
  },
  "include": ["src/**/*", "tests/**/*", "scripts/**/*", "../../types.d.ts"]
}
